Q1
SQL vs NoSQL (Key Differences)

SQL (Relational Databases):

Uses tables (rows and columns)
Fixed schema
Supports joins
Strong ACID properties
Scales vertically
Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL

NoSQL (Non-relational Databases):

Uses flexible formats (JSON, key-value, graph)
Dynamic schema
Limited or no joins
Eventual consistency (BASE)
Scales horizontally
Examples: MongoDB, Redis

Example:

SQL Query:
SELECT * FROM students WHERE gpa > 3.5;

MongoDB Query:
db.students.find({ gpa: { $gt: 3.5 } })

Q2

CAP Theorem

CAP stands for:

Consistency: All nodes see the same data
Availability: Every request gets a response
Partition Tolerance: System works despite network failures

Key Idea:
A distributed system can guarantee only 2 out of these 3 properties.

Reason:
Network failures (partitions) are unavoidable, so the system must choose between:

Consistency (may reject requests)
Availability (may return stale data)

Examples:

MongoDB → CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance)
Cassandra → AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance)
When MongoDB is Preferred
Flexible Schema:
Data structure can vary between documents.

Example:
{ name: "Abhishek", skills: ["JS", "Node"] }
{ name: "Ravi", age: 22 }

High Scalability:
Used in:
Social media apps
Logging systems
E-commerce platforms
Nested / Complex Data:
Supports embedded documents.

Example:
{
name: "Book",
author: {
name: "John",
age: 40
}
}

Q3
Why MongoDB Uses BSON

BSON = Binary JSON

Advantages:

Faster processing (binary format)
Supports more data types (Date, ObjectId, Binary)
Efficient storage

Example:
{
_id: ObjectId("..."),
createdAt: ISODate("2026-04-02")
}

Q4

Find students with GPA > 3.5 and enrolled in CS101:

db.students.find({
gpa: { $gt: 3.5 },
courses: "CS101"
})

If courses is an array:
{
name: "Abhishek",
gpa: 3.8,
courses: ["CS101", "MA101"]
}

Alternative query:
db.students.find({
gpa: { $gt: 3.5 },
courses: { $in: ["CS101"] }
})
